.\" vim: ts=4 et
.TH CRYO 1 "2026-11-17" "cryo 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cryo \- zfs snapshot lifecycle maintenance and replication
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cryo
.RI [ options ]
.I subcommand
.br
.B cryo
.RI [ options ]
.B replicate
.I dataset target
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cryo
takes periodic and ad-hoc snapshots of zfs datasets, manages their
retention, and replicates them to other hosts, pools, or plain files.
It has
.B no configuration files
and no subcommand for editing policy. Everything it does is driven
entirely by
.B cryo:*
zfs user properties, read and written with the ordinary
.BR "zfs get" / "zfs set" / "zfs inherit" ,
the same as any other zfs property. There is nothing to install
alongside it, nothing to keep in sync, and nothing that goes stale
when a pool moves to another machine \(em the policy travels with the
pool.
.PP
.B cryo
is fully automated: it discovers what to act on by walking
.B cryo:*
properties across every imported pool on the host. A dataset with none
set, directly or by inheritance, is simply left alone. There is no
flag or argument anywhere in
.BR cryo 's
own command line for naming a dataset to act on \(em the properties
.I are
the selection mechanism.
.PP
This page documents how to drive an already-installed
.BR cryo .
It does not explain why it's built the way it is; that rationale,
along with the wire protocol and claims-model internals, lives in the
.B cryo.md
design document shipped alongside it (see
.B FILES
below).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-man
Display this manual page: the embedded roff source, formatted and
piped through a pager, if standard output is a terminal; otherwise the
raw roff source (suitable for redirecting to a
.B .1
file) is written to standard output and nothing is formatted.
.TP
.BR \-d ",  \fB\-\-debug\fR"
Enable debugging output.
.TP
.BR \-V ",  \fB\-\-version\fR"
Print the version number and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ",  \fB\-\-help\fR"
Print a short usage summary and exit.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B daemon
Run continuously (the normal way to run
.BR cryo ).
Keeps every discovered policy, schedule, and snapshot claim in memory;
computes the single earliest next action across everything it manages
and sleeps until then, rather than polling on a fixed interval; and
answers
.BR run / status / resync / stop
from any other
.B cryo
invocation over a local control socket, from that same cached state,
instead of re-scanning zfs for every request. See
.BR "EXECUTION MODEL" .
.TP
.B run
Evaluate whatever's due right now \(em snapshots to take, claims to
expire, replication targets to catch up \(em across everything
discovered, then exit. Prefers a running daemon's cached state if one
is reachable (asking it to do the work and reporting what it did);
otherwise scans zfs directly and does the work itself. This is the
fallback for driving
.B cryo
from
.BR cron (8)
instead of running it as a daemon.
.TP
.B status
Read-only report of every discovered policy (with its schedule and
whether it's local or inherited), configured limit/reserve, configured
replication target, and existing cryo-managed snapshot with its
current claims. Same daemon-cache-or-direct-scan preference as
.BR run .
Useful on its own, and as a quick check that a property you just set
parsed the way you expected.
.TP
.B resync
Ask a running daemon to immediately re-scan zfs in full, instead of
waiting for its own periodic interval. Only meaningful against a
running daemon; fails if none is reachable. Runs in the background on
the daemon's side \(em
.B resync
returns once that scan completes, but the daemon keeps answering other
requests from its old state in the meantime.
.TP
.B stop
Ask a running daemon to shut down cleanly: it finishes handling this
request, removes its own control socket, and exits. Only meaningful
against a running daemon; fails if none is reachable. This is the
graceful-shutdown path \(em
.B cryo daemon
has no signal handling of its own, so a plain
.BR kill (1)
just terminates it mid-whatever-it-was-doing instead.
.TP
.BI replicate " dataset target"
One-shot replication: sends
.I dataset Ns' s
latest cryo-managed snapshot (incrementally, if the two ends share a
common one) to
.IR target ,
specified directly on the command line rather than read from a
.B cryo:target
property.
.I target
uses exactly the same grammar as that property's value; see
.BR "REPLICATION TARGETS" .
Useful for a first sync, a manual catch-up, or testing a target spec
before committing it to a property.
.TP
.B server
Speaks
.BR cryo 's
wire protocol on stdin/stdout, as the receiving side of a replication
session. This is what the ssh/pipe/local transports invoke on the
other end \(em there's no reason to run it by hand.
.SH "SNAPSHOT POLICIES"
A snapshot policy is a
.B cryo:snapshot
property, or
.BR cryo:snapshot\- Ns\fIname\fR
for an additional, independently-scheduled policy on the same
dataset (any number of letters/digits/underscores for
.IR name ).
A dataset can carry as many of these as needed \(em a baseline
.B cryo:snapshot
plus, say, a
.B cryo:snapshot\-daily
and a
.BR cryo:snapshot\-offsite .
Like any zfs property, a policy set higher in the dataset tree is
inherited by everything below it until a descendant sets (or
.BR "zfs inherit" s)
its own.
.PP
A policy's value is
.IB frequency / retention \fR.
.TP
.I frequency
One or more number-letter pairs with no separator: \fB15m\fR,
\fB1h\fR, \fB1w3d\fR. Units are
.B m
(minutes),
.B h
(hours),
.B d
(days), and
.B w
(weeks) only \(em nothing else is accepted, because every other unit is
either too short to matter or ambiguous (a month or a year is not a
fixed number of seconds).
.TP
.I retention
Either a bare integer (keep that many of the most recent snapshots
this policy still claims), a duration in the same notation as
.IR frequency \fR
(keep whatever's within that age), or the literal word
.B indefinite
(synonym:
.BR * ),
meaning keep forever \(em subject to the space-pressure carve\-out in
.BR "SPACE LIMITS" .
.PP
If more than one policy on a dataset comes due at the same moment,
.B cryo
takes a single snapshot that satisfies all of them, not one per
policy. A snapshot is destroyed exactly when
.I no
policy still needs it to exist; as long as even one policy's retention
still covers it, it stays, regardless of what any other policy claiming
it would have decided on its own.
.PP
Examples:
.nf
.ft B
zfs set cryo:snapshot=15m/8h tank/data
zfs set cryo:snapshot\-daily=1d/30 tank/data
zfs set cryo:snapshot\-archive=1w/indefinite tank/data
zfs inherit cryo:snapshot\-daily tank/data/scratch
.ft R
.fi
The first line snapshots every 15 minutes and keeps 8 hours' worth. The
second, once a day, keeping the most recent 30. The third, weekly,
forever (space permitting). The fourth opts
.B tank/data/scratch
back out of the inherited daily policy, falling back to whatever
.B tank/data
itself would otherwise have been inheriting for that name from further
up the tree.
.PP
Snapshots are named
.BI dataset @cryo\- YYYYMMDD.HHMMSS
\(em the name carries no policy identity, only when it was taken, since
what matters for keeping it around is never "which cadence produced
this" but "does anything still need it." A manually-created snapshot
named
.BI dataset @cryo\-instant\- YYYYMMDD.HHMMSS
(create it yourself with
.BR "zfs snapshot" ;
.B cryo
has no command of its own for this, by the same no-dataset-argument
design as everything else) is recognized but never auto-expired \(em
delete it yourself with
.B "zfs destroy"
when it's no longer needed.
.SH "SPACE LIMITS"
Two independent, optional escape valves exist for
.B indefinite
retention, so it doesn't have to mean "fill the disk":
.TP
.B cryo:limit
A dataset property (inheritable, like everything else): a size ceiling
on that dataset plus all its own snapshots. Once exceeded,
.B cryo
destroys snapshots oldest-first \(em but only ones every remaining
claim on which is
.BR indefinite ;
one still covered by a time- or count-based policy is never touched
this way, no matter how far over the limit the dataset is.
.TP
.B cryo:reserve
A pool property (read only from the pool's own root dataset \(em
setting it on a child has no effect, even though it's visible there
too by ordinary inheritance). A minimum amount of free space to
maintain on the whole pool. When it's specified as a single value,
that's both the trigger and the target: free space dropping below it
starts cleanup, which continues until free space is back at or above
it. Specified as a
.IB high : low
pair instead, the smaller value
.RI ( low )
is the trigger and the larger
.RI ( high )
is the target \(em cleanup runs further than the bare minimum,
on purpose, so it doesn't immediately re-trigger the moment it stops.
Pressure from
.B cryo:reserve
is pool-wide: the next snapshot it reclaims is the oldest eligible one
.I anywhere in the pool
it applies to, not the oldest within whichever dataset happens to be
over some local limit.
.PP
Sizes take an optional suffix: lowercase is decimal (\fBk\fR=1000,
\fBm\fR=10\(ha6, \fBg\fR=10\(ha9, \fBt\fR=10\(ha12); uppercase is binary
(\fBK\fR=1024, \fBM\fR=1024\(ha2, \fBG\fR=1024\(ha3, \fBT\fR=1024\(ha4);
no suffix means bytes.
.nf
.ft B
zfs set cryo:limit=500G tank/data
zfs set cryo:reserve=50G tank
zfs set cryo:reserve=60G:40G tank
.ft R
.fi
.SH "REPLICATION TARGETS"
A replication target is a
.B cryo:target
property, or
.BR cryo:target\- Ns\fIname\fR
for an additional one on the same dataset \(em same naming rules and
inheritance as snapshot policies. Every target configured on a
dataset is kept caught up automatically, every cycle a daemon or a
.B run
evaluates that dataset: whatever cryo-managed snapshot is newest gets
sent, incrementally if the two ends already share a common one,
whenever it isn't already there. There's no separate schedule for
replication \(em it simply tracks whatever snapshot policy produces.
.PP
A target's value is
.RB [ endpoint : ] path
.RB [ transport : detail ].
.TP
.I path
If it begins with a letter, it's a zfs dataset path on the target side
(local or remote, per
.IR endpoint ).
If it begins with
.B .
or
.BR / ,
it's a filesystem location instead: see
.B "VFS (FILE) TARGETS"
below. If omitted entirely, the target uses the same path as the
source dataset.
.TP
.I endpoint
A hostname, or an alias from
.BR ssh_config (5).
If present, the default transport is ssh, invoking
.B "ssh " Ns\fIendpoint\fR " cryo server"
on the other end \(em tune ssh's own behavior (keys, ports, jump
hosts, keepalives) through
.B ~/.ssh/config
rather than through
.BR cryo .
If absent, the target is local, and no transport is needed at all.
.TP
.I transport
Normally inferred from whether
.I endpoint
is present (ssh if so, local if not), but can be given explicitly
after a space:
.BR ssh: ,
.BI pipe: command
(runs
.I command
as a shell command line that's expected to connect to a
.B "cryo server"
on the other end \(em useful for a jump host, a VPN, or anything
.B ssh
alone can't express), or
.BR local: .
.PP
Examples:
.nf
.ft B
zfs set cryo:target=backup/data tank/data
zfs set cryo:target\-offsite=backuphost:archive/data tank/data
zfs set cryo:target\-relay='relay:archive/data pipe:ssh \-J jump relay cryo server' tank/data
.ft R
.fi
.PP
An encrypted source dataset is always sent raw
.RB ( "zfs send \-w" ) :
the target never needs the encryption key for anything
.B cryo
does to it, including its own snapshot lifecycle management on the
replica side. Two consequences of that are expected behavior, not
bugs: a key rotation
.RB ( "zfs change\-key" )
forces the next replication of that dataset to be a fresh full send
rather than an incremental one, and the replica is not independently
readable without the key being supplied there separately.
.PP
An interrupted transfer resumes automatically on the next attempt,
using zfs's own resume tokens, rather than restarting from scratch \(em
there's nothing to configure for this; it's detected and used whenever
one is pending.
.PP
A replication target records where it came from in a
.B cryo:origin
property, set on the replica dataset and its snapshots, carrying the
source's real hostname and the source pool's and dataset's zfs GUIDs.
If a later replication attempt's identity doesn't match what's already
recorded there, it's refused outright \(em most likely a
misconfiguration, a rebuilt source, or an unrelated dataset that
happens to share a name, never silently overwritten.
.B cryo
never snapshots a dataset of non-local origin on its own initiative; it
still manages the lifecycle of whatever snapshots are already on it,
per whatever policy properties are set there (typically its
.I own
cryo instance's policy, configured separately from whatever produced
them on the source side).
.SH "VFS (FILE) TARGETS"
When a target's path begins with
.B .
or
.BR / ,
there's no
.B "cryo server"
on the other end at all \(em
.B cryo
writes the raw
.B "zfs send"
stream straight to a file:
.IB path /dataset/ snapshot-name\fR.zfs\fR,
mirroring the dataset's own path as nested directories. Before sending
anything, it looks at what's already in that directory (instead of
asking a peer) to find the newest snapshot both ends already have, for
an incremental send; a file is written first under a
.B .partial
sibling name and renamed into place only once the send has actually
finished, so an interrupted write can never be mistaken later for a
valid baseline. These files are ordinary zfs send streams \(em replay
one anywhere with a plain
.BR "zfs receive" .
There's no peer to check identity against, so none of the
.B cryo:origin
tracking above applies on this path.
.SH "EXECUTION MODEL"
.B cryo daemon
is the normal way to run this: a single long-lived process, with no
double-fork/detach of its own (whatever starts it \(em a service
manager, typically \(em owns backgrounding it). On startup it scans
every imported pool once, then computes the earliest next action across
every policy it found and sleeps until then, rather than polling
blindly. It re-scans from scratch periodically on a fixed safety-net
interval regardless of anything else, and optionally wakes early on a
.BR zpool (8)
events hook so a
.B "zfs set cryo:..."
is picked up promptly \(em but that's purely a responsiveness
optimization, never something correctness depends on.
.PP
A running daemon exposes a local control socket that every other
.B cryo
invocation checks first.
.BR run / status
become small requests answered from the daemon's cached state instead
of a fresh scan, if one is reachable there; otherwise they fall back to
scanning and acting directly, exactly as if no daemon were running at
all.
.BR resync / stop
only ever make sense against a running daemon and fail cleanly if
none is reachable.
.PP
.B cryo run
is the alternative for anyone who'd rather drive this from
.BR cron (8)
than run a persistent process: it does one pass of whatever's due,
then exits, with no socket and no caching involved.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.ft B
# set a baseline policy and watch it get discovered correctly
zfs set cryo:snapshot=1h/24 tank/data
cryo status

# run the daemon in a terminal to watch it work (normally a service manager does this)
cryo daemon

# ask it to pick up a property change immediately, rather than waiting
cryo resync

# shut the daemon down cleanly
cryo stop

# drive everything from cron instead
*/15 * * * *  cryo run

# add a replication target, then prove it out once by hand before relying on it
zfs set cryo:target=backuphost:archive/data tank/data
cryo replicate tank/data backuphost:archive/data
.ft R
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CRYO_SOCKET
Overrides the local control socket path a daemon binds (and every
other invocation looks for first). Defaults to
.BI $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR /cryo.sock
if that's set, otherwise
.BI /tmp/cryo\- user .sock\fR.
A packaged systemd unit typically sets this explicitly to a
service-managed path such as
.BR /run/cryo/cryo.sock .
.SH FILES
None \(em by design.
.B cryo
has no configuration files; all configuration and state lives in
.B cryo:*
zfs user properties, which travel with the pool itself. The one
runtime file it creates is the control socket named by
.B CRYO_SOCKET
above, removed again on a clean
.BR "cryo stop" .
.PP
If installed alongside the standard documentation path, the full
design document \(em the wire protocol, the claims-model internals,
and the reasoning behind every choice summarized in this page \(em is
at
.BR /usr/local/share/doc/cryo/cryo.md " (or the equivalent under whatever " \-\-prefix " it was installed with)."
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.I nonzero
Failure: a property failed to parse, a zfs command failed, a
requested daemon
.RB ( resync / stop )
wasn't reachable, or a replication attempt failed. The message on
standard error describes which.
.SH "PRIVILEGE AND SECURITY"
.B cryo
has no authorization layer of its own. The entire trust boundary is
external to it: whoever can reach
.B "cryo server"
(by ssh, by a pipe transport, or locally) as a given user, and whatever
that user's
.BR "zfs allow" (8)
or sudo grants permit, is implicitly authorized to do whatever
.B cryo
does on their behalf. Running the daemon as root is simplest when it's
meant to discover and manage every pool on a host; delegating specific
permissions
.RB ( create ", " destroy ", " mount ", " receive ", "
.RB  rollback ", " send ", " snapshot ", " userprop )
to an unprivileged user via
.B "zfs allow"
on just the pools it should touch is the alternative, if running
as root isn't acceptable.
